WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.490
INTRO:
We all know the saying just buy and hold.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:08.129
Whether the market goes up, down, sideways,
or in circles, you should forget about the

00:00:08.129 --> 00:00:12.610
noise and dollar cost average into index funds
and blue chip companies.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.550
While this strategy is a piece of cake to
follow during bull markets, it often gets

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.020
a lot harder during bear markets.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:25.340
You might hold through a 20, 30, 40% correction,
but at some point, most people feel that continuing

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.050
to invest is simply hopeless.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.939
They’ve seen the market tank for weeks if
not months on end due to loads of selling

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:32.939
pressure.

00:00:32.939 --> 00:00:38.000
So clearly, someone is cutting losses and
getting the opportunity buy back in lower.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.750
Seeing this, they stop buying or they sell
their positions as well to hopefully buy back

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:43.760
lower.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.260
But for some reason, the stock just starts
skyrocketing the moment they sell.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.730
A perfect example of this was with Jack from
the Iced Coffee Hour.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:57.330
On May 12, 2022, Jack announced that he had
to sell all of his robinhood stock because

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:09.200
he got margin called.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:20.850
Now, Graham was super excited because he has
a superstition that Jack selling is great

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:21.920
for the market.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.150
And while this may seem like a silly belief,
somehow Robinhood stock skyrocketed 38.4%

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.920
the day this video was posted.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.510
Now, Robinhood is still in an overall downtrend,
so for all we know, it may have months if

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.100
not years of downside left.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.220
But, it’s still quite ashtonishing that
the stock made such a move the moment that

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.420
Jack sold.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.259
However, this isn’t just some random coincidence.

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:50.829
In fact, most retail investors experience
this same phenomenon during every market sell

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:51.829
off.

00:01:51.829 --> 00:01:55.899
They might’ve been diamond handing for months,
but the second they sell is the bottom.

00:01:55.899 --> 00:02:01.439
So, why do stocks always skyrocket the moment
you sell?

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:07.430
SMART MONEY:
To understand why the stock market seems to

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:11.670
always work against you, we have to first
understand who controls the market.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.680
Whether you like it or not, the stock market
and most financial markets are controlled

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.650
by large institutional investors like market
makers, hedge funds, and investment bankers.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.750
These guys use their money to create various
emotions within the market and then they use

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.760
these emotions against the average investor.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.140
Their strategy hasn’t really changed in
decades, but really, there’s no need for

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.989
them to even change their strategy.

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:37.300
Here’s the thing, while the asset that people
or buying or the medium that they’re buying

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.870
it through may change, people’s psychology
remains constant.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:46.480
So regardless of the time period or situation,
we see the same cycles playout over and over

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.480
again.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:51.379
Every bull market starts out with a stealth
phase where smart money buys into a promising

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:52.379
investment.

00:02:52.379 --> 00:02:55.720
This initial phase of smart money investors
usually doesn’t include the big hedge funds

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.129
or market makers.

00:02:57.129 --> 00:03:01.760
Rather, this phase is filled with innovators,
entrepreneurs, and maybe a bit of venture

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:02.769
capital.

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:07.420
For example, Steve Jobs starting Apple in
the 1970s or Jeff Bezos starting Amazon in

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.610
the 1990s would be considered a smart money
investment.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:15.900
In most cases, the underlying investment is
truly a good purchase, but the problem is

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.569
that it doesn’t take too long for the investment
to get ahead of itself.

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:23.450
Once the innovators get the business off the
ground, we see institutional investors flood

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:24.450
in.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:29.269
This is where the legendary investors generally
buy in like Warren Buffett and Peter Lynch.

00:03:29.269 --> 00:03:33.720
A lot of companies also tend to invest during
this time period.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.920
For example, Ford and Amazon invested into
Rivian during this phase.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.819
Once all the institutional investors are in,
they start going on TV and talking about their

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:44.930
investments in these companies, and this is
when the average retail investor first hears

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.379
about this investment.

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:51.490
If they went ahead and bought instantly, they
would actually do quite well, but most retail

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.931
investors are too level headed at this point
to make such a decision.

00:03:54.931 --> 00:03:58.780
They might look at the financial metrics of
a company or their earnings reports.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.569
And though they might like the investment
itself, they usually won’t pull the trigger

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:05.810
because they don’t feel the FOMO yet or
the fear of missing out.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:09.379
Their level headed analysis usually tells
them that the investment is selling for a

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:13.300
premium and that they should be able to get
it for cheaper in the future.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.660
Meanwhile, the more sophisticated retail investors
who’s well aware of these cycles knows that

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.680
a cheaper opportunity may not come, so they
go ahead and buy in.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.970
A great example of this in the real world
is Tesla stock during mid 2020.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.320
At the time, the stock was selling at about
$200 per share, and while this may seem like

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.030
a great deal in retrospect, $200 per share
was already paying a premium given that the

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:38.970
stock was at $60 not long before this.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:42.940
These guys won’t have to wait too long to
make money though as the party is just getting

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.490
started.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.620
RETAIL MANIA:
After the sophisticated investors buy in,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.090
we see the degenerates join the party during
the enthusiasm phase.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:57.500
These guys generally have no idea what they’re
doing as they’re not exactly investing based

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.100
on logic or reason.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:03.740
Usually, it’s just a case of, they saw some
guy on YouTube or TikTok make a lot of money

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.830
with the investment, so they wanted to join
the party.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:11.530
A great example of this is buying doge between
10 and 50 cents.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:15.919
Meanwhile, the average retail investor knows
that such ludicrous moves aren’t sustainable

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:20.120
and that it’s just a matter of time until
these degenerates get destroyed.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:25.530
But, at the same time, they also see these
gamblers post insane gains on social media

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.840
which gives them a slight sense of FOMO.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.460
Shortly after, we move into the greed phase,
and this is when elephants like Michael Burry

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.550
and Jeremy Grantham wake up and start calling
the market a bubble.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:40.640
These voices of reason calms down the average
retail investor and justifies their choice

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:41.700
to sit out.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.380
But, despite their grand predictions of an
epic collapse, the market will continue to

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.039
roar on for years, and this is when the average
investor really starts to feel the FOMO.

00:05:52.039 --> 00:05:54.500
What if the market crash never comes?

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.610
What if this is the new normal?

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.870
What if this time is different?

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:03.380
If you ever see yourself asking this question,
you should instantly stop yourself and not

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:07.280
do what you’re wanting to do because this
time is never different.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:12.120
But, despite this, as we enter the delusion
phase of the market, all signs will be pointing

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:17.970
towards this time being different, and unfortunately
most retail investors will give into the FOMO

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.300
and buy in.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.419
At first, they may even be up for a month
or two as we seemingly move into a new paradigm,

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:27.570
but it doesn’t take long for smart money
to dumping their positions.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:32.560
You see, the influx of retail investors gives
big investors the volume they need to offload

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.220
their massive positions without hurting the
market too much.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:39.910
Even with all the new retail volume though,
the investment will sell off a good amount

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:43.290
due to the big investors selling which brings
us into the denial phase.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:49.229
At this point, the average investor isn’t
down that much, maybe 10 or 20%.

00:06:49.229 --> 00:06:51.490
But the first inklings of fear start to set
in.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:55.289
Fortunately, some late retail investors who
didn’t buy in during the delusion phase

00:06:55.289 --> 00:06:59.069
will buy right now thinking that they’re
getting a decent discount.

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:02.349
This buying pressure will cause the investment
to rally back to where the average retail

00:07:02.349 --> 00:07:06.280
investor bought in, and people will think
that we’re finally returning to normal.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:11.599
But, in reality, the pain hasn’t even begun.

00:07:11.599 --> 00:07:15.900
DEATH SPIRAL:
As the market puts in a lower high and volume

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.620
starts to dry up, some more sophisticated
retail investors will start exiting their

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.910
positions.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:24.370
This would be equivalent to when Meet Kevin
sold all of his positions near the beginning

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:25.370
of the year.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:29.479
He didn’t sell at the absolute top, but
he sold before the vast majority of the pain

00:07:29.479 --> 00:07:30.550
showed up.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:35.570
And as these investors start offloading the
positions and no new buyers show up, the investment

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.650
starts to sell off once again leading to market
fear.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:42.780
While the average investor is feeling quite
fearful at this point, they feel that their

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.990
too deep to cash out now.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:49.780
They might already be down 40 or 50%, and
they feel that things can’t get much worse

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:50.780
right?

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:55.979
Well, they can and that’s exactly what happens
next as the market capitulates.

00:07:55.979 --> 00:08:00.860
This is when we see those 70 to 80% crashes
like we saw in Netflix and PayPal.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:05.290
During this captiulation, the vast majority
of retail investors will sell out either due

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.539
to extreme fear or being margin called.

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:10.750
And the people who hold on will enter a phase
of despair.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:16.280
A perfect example of this in the real world
is when Bitcoin settled down at $3000 to $4000

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.060
after running to $20k.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.819
During this phase, your investment willing
seemingly have no hope and you’ll probably

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:24.330
be made fun of for holding such an investment.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:30.050
In reality, this is the best time to be buying
in, but no one will care to do so.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.080
And the few remaining retail investors will
finally give in and cash out the little they

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.080
have left.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:39.570
As the last retail investors are flushed out,
a new set of smart money will become interested

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:40.830
once again.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:45.760
The most recent example of this is FTX founder
Sam Bankman buying into Robinhood at basically

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:46.760
book value.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:51.680
So, it’s not a coincidence that you always
end up buying the top and selling the bottom.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.700
Through every step in the market cycle, smart
money manipulate the market and your emotions

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:57.390
to make you do exactly this.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:02.420
They buy right after retail investors are
flushed out and the sell right when retail

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:08.490
investors reenter the market and they repeat
this over and over and over again.

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:13.240
HUMAN PSYCHOLOGY:
Something else to consider is that even if

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:17.540
you don’t get crushed by market cycles like
the average investor, it’s likely that you’ll

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:22.170
still have this same feeling that stocks skyrocket
the moment you sell due to human psychology.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:26.370
Our brains are wired to remember the times
that we messed up or missed out on something.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:29.700
Meanwhile, we forget all the times that we
made the right call.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:34.970
A personal example of this happening to me
is during the Gamestop run in early 2021.

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:40.070
I became aware of the Gamestop situation relatively
early when the stock was at about $60.

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:45.720
So, I put $840 into a degenerate call and
it actually ended up working out.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:49.570
The following week, I upped the anti a bit
and bought some more degenerate calls for

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:51.290
$6500.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:55.370
Within a matter of hours, this position skyrocketed
to $14,750.

00:09:55.370 --> 00:10:00.810
I was torn between swinging the position overnight
or just locking in the profits.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.470
I ended up playing it safe and locking in
the profits.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:07.880
But, just a few hours after I sold, Elon Musk
would tweet Gamestonk and the stock would

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:09.910
moon to nearly $500.

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:14.950
If I had decided to simply swing the degenerate
calls over night, I would’ve made a $100,000

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:17.829
as I would’ve definitely sold the first
thing the next morning.

00:10:17.829 --> 00:10:21.230
And looking back, this puts me in a really
ironic position.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:26.660
Instead of being grateful that I made $8250
which is a phenomenal amount or being thankful

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:31.990
that I didn’t buy the top, I’m over here
being salty that I didn’t make another $80,000.

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:35.900
Now on the other hand, what if Gamestop had
crashed right after I sold?

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:40.680
I probably would’ve written off my degenerate
trade as a good call and forgotten about it.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:45.130
But since I sold right before such a monumental
event took place, the trade has become something

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:47.370
that I’ll probably never forget.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:51.370
And I bet that this same sentiment applies
to most investors as well.

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:55.910
We’ll forget about the dozens or hundreds
of times that we made the right call by selling.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:59.490
But you can bet that we’ll remember that
few time that we missed out.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:03.460
And psychologically, these few times will
overshadow all of our great calls making it

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:08.019
seem like we always sell right before stocks
skyrocket when in reality, that’s simply

00:11:08.019 --> 00:11:10.430
not the case.

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:15.209
FIGHTING PSYCHOLOGY:
At the end of the day, market cycles and human

00:11:15.209 --> 00:11:18.310
psychology are extremely difficult to navigate.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:21.980
Even if you’re aware of all this, it’s
basically a coin flip when it comes to making

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.040
the right decision when the time comes.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:28.579
And while some investors have managed to overcome
these challenges, the average investor will

00:11:28.579 --> 00:11:33.310
never overcome these mind games and that’s
why dollar cost averaging is by far the best

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:35.460
strategy for all of us.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:39.290
Even though there’s so much noise within
each market cycle, one thing that remains

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:42.760
true is that the mean is constantly increasing.

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:46.339
And as long as you’re putting money into
fundamentally strong investments, the mean

00:11:46.339 --> 00:11:48.649
should only go up over the long term.

00:11:48.649 --> 00:11:53.160
Also, by dollar cost averaging the same amount
of money on a regular basis, you’d naturally

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.810
be buying more shares when the market is down
and less shares when the market is up.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:00.760
And you didn’t even have to spend any effort
in trying to time the bottom or top.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.779
So, the next time you feel like stocks moon
right after you sell or that stocks crash

00:12:04.779 --> 00:12:08.990
right after you buy, this should be a strong
signal that you should be timing the market

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:13.480
less and dollar cost averaging more, but that’s
just what I think.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:16.140
Have you guys ever sold the bottom or bought
the top?

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:17.399
Comment that down below.

00:12:17.399 --> 00:12:20.589
Also, drop a like if you’re a fan of dollar
cost averaging.

